# --------------------------------------------------------------------- #
# safety rules / essays (fixed prose)
# --------------------------------------------------------------------- #
RULES = [
    "The first rule of mushroom foraging is simple and absolute: never eat a mushroom unless you are one hundred percent certain of its identity. A mistake can mean anything from a bad night of vomiting to permanent liver damage or death. There is no home test, folk trick or cooking method that reliably makes a poisonous mushroom safe, and there is no substitute for careful, systematic identification.",
    "Beginners should learn the deadly mushrooms first, before learning any edible ones. The death cap, the destroying angel and the autumn skullcap account for most fatal poisonings worldwide, and all of them have edible-looking relatives. If you can reliably recognize the amatoxin mushrooms, you have already eliminated the most dangerous category from your basket.",
    "When you pick a mushroom, collect the whole fruit body, including the base of the stem. Many critical identification features live at the bottom: the cup-shaped volva of the deadly Amanitas, the bulbous base, the color of the stem base, and the way it attaches to wood or soil. A mushroom cut off at ground level is a mushroom that cannot be identified safely.",
    "A spore print is one of the most useful tools in mushroom identification, and it costs nothing. Cut the cap off and lay it gill-side down on a piece of white paper and half of a second piece of dark paper, cover it with a bowl, and wait a few hours. The color of the released spores — white, pink, brown, black, green — is often the single fastest way to separate an edible from a poisonous lookalike.",
    "The color of the spore print divides the mushroom world into useful groups: white and pale spores include the Amanitas and many Russulas; pink spores include Pluteus, Entoloma and Volvariella; brown spores include the deadly Galerina; black spores include the inkcaps; and a green spore print is the signature of the poisonous Chlorophyllum molybdites.",
    "Amanita species are the single most dangerous family for beginners because so many of them are deadly and so many resemble familiar edible mushrooms. The rule for novices is blunt: avoid mushrooms with white gills, a ring on the stem and a cup-shaped volva at the base. That combination describes the deadliest mushrooms on earth.",
    "Every year, people are poisoned by mushrooms they have eaten for years, because a similar-looking species fruited in the same spot. A mushroom's identity is not fixed by the place where it grows, the season, or the tree it grows under. Re-identify every single mushroom every single time, no matter how familiar it looks.",
    "The 'little brown mushrooms' — small, brown, with gills — are responsible for a disproportionate share of poisonings because they are so hard to tell apart. Some of them, such as Galerina marginata, are deadly. The practical advice is to skip small brown mushrooms entirely until you have years of experience with the genus.",
    "Poisonous mushrooms do not announce themselves. They do not turn silver spoons black, they do not taste bad, they do not have to be brightly colored, and they do not only grow in dark woods. The green-spored parasol grows on suburban lawns; the death cap grows under park oaks. The only reliable test is identification, not folklore.",
    "Cooking destroys some toxins and not others. Amatoxins and orellanine survive boiling, frying and drying; gyromitrin is volatile and can even poison through fumes; muscarine survives cooking. The idea that 'cooking makes it safe' is dangerously wrong for the most lethal mushrooms.",
    "If you suspect mushroom poisoning, act immediately and do not wait for symptoms: call a poison control center, save every piece of the uneaten mushroom (and any vomit) for identification, and seek medical care even if the person feels fine. The most dangerous toxins have a delayed onset of many hours, and early treatment is the difference between recovery and permanent damage.",
    "Do not rely on apps, photographs or memory for the final identification of a mushroom you intend to eat. Use them as a first step, then confirm with a field guide, a spore print, and ideally an experienced local forager. The cost of a wrong answer is too high for shortcuts.",
    "The golden rule of the forager's basket: when in doubt, throw it out. A single questionable mushroom can contaminate an entire meal, and no dish is worth a hospital stay. The best foragers are the most conservative ones.",
    "Mushrooms sold in shops are safe because they are cultivated or professionally selected. The white button mushroom, shiitake, oyster, enoki and king oyster that you buy in a supermarket are grown from controlled spawn on sterile substrates. Wild mushrooms are a completely different category and must be treated with respect.",
    "Children and pets are especially at risk from mushrooms in gardens and parks, because they may eat mushrooms out of curiosity. Check lawns before letting toddlers play, remove unknown mushrooms from the yard, and teach children the rule that wild mushrooms are not food.",
    "Some edible mushrooms cause reactions only in certain people. Shiitake can cause a rash in sensitive individuals; chicken of the woods can cause lip swelling and nausea in a minority of eaters; honey fungus upsets some stomachs even when cooked. When trying any new wild mushroom for the first time, eat a small amount and wait.",
    "A mushroom that grows on wood, such as the deadly Galerina or the toxic sulfur tuft, can look surprisingly similar to the edible honey fungus and velvet shank. Wood-rotting mushrooms require the same rigorous identification as ground mushrooms: check the gill color, the ring, the spore print and the exact host tree.",
    "Mushroom identification is a skill built from many observations: cap shape and color, gill attachment and color, stem features, odor, taste (a small nibble of a known edible or of a bitter non-toxic species is a standard field test), spore print, habitat, host tree, and season. No single feature is enough.",
    "The most famous culinary mushrooms — porcini, chanterelles, morels, truffles — all have toxic or mediocre lookalikes. The porcini has the bitter bolete; the chanterelle has the jack-o'-lantern; the morel has the false morel; the truffle has false truffles. Excellence in the kitchen does not replace excellence in identification.",
    "Foraging etiquette and ecology matter as much as safety: pick only what you will use, cut rather than tear when possible, leave the mycelium in the ground, respect private land and protected areas, and never collect rare species. A good forager leaves the forest as healthy as it was found.",
]

# --------------------------------------------------------------------- #
# FAQ (short Q&A prose)
# --------------------------------------------------------------------- #
FAQ = [
    ("Are mushrooms with a ring safe to eat?", "No. A ring on the stem is a feature of many deadly Amanita species, including the death cap and destroying angel, as well as the deadly Galerina. It is also found on perfectly good mushrooms such as the button mushroom and honey fungus, so a ring alone proves nothing either way. It is simply one feature to record, never a safety guarantee."),
    ("Do poisonous mushrooms turn silver spoons black?", "No. This is one of the most widespread myths about mushrooms. The silver-spoon test does not detect mushroom toxins and gives people a false sense of security. The only reliable protection is correct identification."),
    ("Can I identify a mushroom by tasting it?", "A tiny nibble and spit is a standard field test used by experienced foragers to detect bitterness or acridity in boletes and russulas, but it is only useful for species known to be non-toxic in that role. Never taste an unknown mushroom, and never swallow."),
    ("Are brightly colored mushrooms always poisonous?", "No. Many brightly colored mushrooms are excellent food: the golden chanterelle, the orange Caesar's mushroom, the red cinnabar chanterelle. Conversely, some of the deadliest mushrooms are dull olive or pure white. Color is a weak clue on its own."),
    ("Do mushrooms growing on wood have different risks?", "Yes. Wood-rotting mushrooms include the delicious oyster and honey fungus, but also the deadly autumn skullcap and the toxic sulfur tuft. The same identification discipline applies: check gill color, ring, spore print and host tree."),
    ("What is the most dangerous mushroom in the world?", "By death toll, the death cap (Amanita phalloides), which contains amatoxins that destroy the liver and is responsible for most fatal poisonings worldwide. The poison fire coral (Podostroma cornu-damae) and the orellanine webcaps are also exceptionally dangerous."),
    ("Why do mushroom poisonings often have delayed symptoms?", "The most lethal toxins act on the liver and kidneys, not the stomach, and take hours to be absorbed and processed. Amatoxin symptoms appear after 6 to 24 hours, and orellanine symptoms after days to weeks. By the time symptoms appear, the toxin has already caused serious damage."),
    ("How long after eating a poisonous mushroom do symptoms appear?", "It depends on the toxin: muscarine and gastrointestinal irritants act within 30 minutes to 3 hours; amatoxins take 6 to 24 hours; gyromitrin 5 to 10 hours; orellanine anywhere from 2 days to 3 weeks. In general, the longer the delay, the more dangerous the toxin."),
    ("Is it safe to eat mushrooms picked in my garden?", "Only if you can identify them with certainty. Lawns commonly host the poisonous green-spored parasol and the toxic yellow stainer, and garden mulch is a favorite habitat of the deadly webcap's relatives. Garden mushrooms deserve the same caution as forest mushrooms."),
    ("What should I do if my child eats an unknown mushroom?", "Remove any remaining mushroom from the child's mouth, save a sample of the mushroom for identification, call a poison control center immediately, and do not induce vomiting unless told to. Do not wait for symptoms: the most dangerous toxins act slowly."),
    ("Can drying or freezing make poisonous mushrooms safe?", "No. Amatoxins and orellanine survive drying, freezing, boiling and frying. Only some toxins are heat-sensitive, such as the hemolysins in morels and honey fungus. Preservation changes texture and shelf life, not toxicity."),
    ("Are all boletes safe to eat?", "No, although most are. The rule for boletes is to avoid any with red or orange pore surfaces, any that bruise deep blue rapidly, and any that taste bitter; the devil's bolete and the bitter bolete are the classic examples. The safe field test is a small nibble of the raw cap."),
    ("What is the difference between a mushroom and a toadstool?", "There is no scientific difference. 'Toadstool' is a folk word for a mushroom people believe is poisonous, while 'mushroom' usually implies edibility. Both are simply the fruiting bodies of fungi, and neither word tells you anything about a particular species."),
    ("Do morels need to be cooked?", "Yes, always. Raw or undercooked morels contain heat-sensitive compounds that cause nausea, vomiting and cramps. Cooking thoroughly destroys these compounds. The same applies to the honey fungus."),
    ("Why are puffballs safe but earthballs poisonous?", "Young puffballs have pure white, firm flesh and are edible, while earthballs have dark or yellowish flesh and cause gastrointestinal poisoning. The rule is to cut every puffball-like mushroom in half: pure white flesh only, and never eat one that contains a developing cap and stem inside."),
    ("What is a mushroom spore print used for?", "A spore print reveals the color of the spores, which is a decisive identification feature. For example, a green print identifies the poisonous green-spored parasol, a rusty-brown print can reveal the deadly Galerina, and a pink print confirms the Pluteus and Volvariella genera."),
    ("Can two mushrooms look identical but one be deadly?", "Yes, and this is exactly the danger. The death cap resembles edible straw mushrooms and young puffballs; the destroying angel resembles white button mushrooms; the autumn skullcap resembles edible honey fungus. Identical-looking mushrooms can be separated only by careful inspection of gills, spores, volva and habitat."),
    ("What is the volva and why does it matter?", "The volva is a cup-shaped sac at the base of the stem. It is the remnant of the universal veil that enclosed the young mushroom. A volva is the signature of the Amanita genus, which contains the deadliest mushrooms on earth, so its presence is a strong warning sign."),
    ("Is it legal to pick wild mushrooms?", "It depends on the country, region and land ownership. Many places allow collecting for personal use in public forests with limits, while others prohibit it or require permits. Always check local regulations, and never collect in protected nature reserves or on private land without permission."),
    ("What do chanterelles taste like?", "Golden chanterelles have a delicate, fruity flavor with a faint apricot-like aroma and a firm, meaty texture. They are among the most prized wild mushrooms in Europe and North America, and they dry and rehydrate well."),
    ("How can I tell a real morel from a false morel?", "A real morel has a honeycomb cap of pits and ridges that is fused to the stem and is hollow inside, stem included. A false morel (Gyromitra) has a wrinkled, brain-like cap and a chambered interior, and the thimble morels (Verpa) have a cap attached only at the top. Morels must be cooked; false morels should be avoided entirely."),
    ("Why does the common inkcap react with alcohol?", "The mushroom contains coprine, which blocks the enzyme that breaks down alcohol, causing a buildup of acetaldehyde. The result is flushing, sweating, nausea and palpitations, much like the reaction to the drug disulfiram. The reaction can occur even if alcohol is drunk days later."),
    ("What is the most common cause of mushroom poisoning in North America?", "The green-spored parasol (Chlorophyllum molybdites), which grows on lawns and is frequently mistaken for the edible shaggy parasol. Its gastrointestinal toxins cause violent vomiting and diarrhea in the hours after eating."),
    ("Are supermarket mushrooms safe?", "Yes. Supermarket mushrooms — button, portobello, shiitake, oyster, enoki, king oyster, brown beech — are cultivated from controlled spawn on sterile substrates, which means they contain no wild lookalikes. They are among the safest foods you can buy."),
    ("What should I bring on a mushroom hunt?", "A basket (not a plastic bag, which makes mushrooms sweat), a knife, a brush for cleaning, a field guide specific to your region, paper bags for separating species, a notebook, and a camera. Most importantly, bring a friend who knows more than you do, at least at the start."),
    ("Do mushrooms have nutritional value?", "Yes. Mushrooms are low in calories and fat, contain protein, fiber, B vitamins, selenium and potassium, and are one of the few non-animal sources of vitamin D when exposed to UV light. They are a healthy addition to most diets."),
    ("Can mushrooms be grown at home?", "Yes, and it is safer than foraging. Oyster mushrooms can be grown on straw or coffee grounds in a few weeks; wine caps grow on wood chips in the garden; shiitake grows on hardwood logs. Growing your own mushrooms is a great way to enjoy them with zero identification risk."),
    ("Why are truffles so expensive?", "Truffles grow underground in a mycorrhizal partnership with specific trees and cannot be cultivated like ordinary mushrooms, and they must be located by trained dogs or pigs. The Périgord black truffle and the Italian white truffle are harvested in tiny quantities, which drives prices to thousands of euros per kilogram."),
    ("What is the difference between a gill and a pore surface?", "Gills are the blade-like structures radiating under the cap of agarics such as the button mushroom and Amanita. A pore surface, found on boletes and polypores, looks like a sponge or a series of tiny tubes. The two are completely different spore-bearing surfaces and are a primary identification feature."),
    ("Are jelly fungi edible?", "Most are edible and harmless, including the wood ear and snow fungus used in Asian cuisine, though they are valued more for texture than flavor. A few are tough or tasteless. No jelly fungus is known to be deadly."),
]

