CULTIVATION = [
    "Growing mushrooms at home is far safer than foraging and surprisingly easy. The basic recipe is the same for every species: pasteurize or sterilize a substrate, mix in spawn, keep it moist and at the right temperature, and wait. Oyster mushrooms are the classic first crop, fruiting from straw or even coffee grounds in as little as three weeks.",
    "Mushroom cultivation starts with spawn, a pure culture of the fungus grown on grain. Spawn is the mushroom equivalent of seeds. Once the mycelium has colonized the grain, it is mixed into the bulk substrate, where it spreads through the material before being triggered to fruit by fresh air, light and a drop in temperature or humidity.",
    "Oyster mushrooms (Pleurotus ostreatus) are the easiest mushrooms to grow. They colonize straw, sawdust, paper and coffee grounds quickly, tolerate a wide temperature range, and produce clusters of grey caps within days of the fruiting trigger. They are the recommended first crop for any home grower.",
    "Shiitake (Lentinula edodes) is grown on hardwood logs or supplemented sawdust blocks. Log cultivation takes six months to two years, but produces flushes of high-quality mushrooms for several years. The traditional method uses freshly cut oak logs, inoculated with plug spawn, and left to colonize in a shady spot.",
    "Wine cap mushrooms (Stropharia rugosoannulata) are grown outdoors on wood chips and garden mulch. Once established, they persist for years and even help break down the chips into compost. They are one of the few mushrooms that can be grown in a vegetable garden with almost no equipment.",
    "Button mushrooms (Agaricus bisporus) are grown on composted manure and straw in dark, cool rooms, which is why they were historically cultivated in caves. The compost must be pasteurized to kill competing molds, and the crop is cased with a layer of peat to trigger fruiting.",
    "The king oyster (Pleurotus eryngii) and beech mushrooms (Hypsizygus tessellatus) are cultivated on supplemented sawdust blocks in climate-controlled rooms. They are among the most valuable cultivated mushrooms because of their firm texture and long shelf life.",
    "Truffles cannot be grown like ordinary mushrooms; instead, growers plant oak or hazel seedlings that have been inoculated with truffle mycorrhiza, then wait five to ten years for the trees to start producing. Truffle orchards are a long-term investment, and the harvest is located with trained dogs.",
    "Temperature and humidity are the two master controls of mushroom cultivation. Most species fruit when the temperature drops slightly and humidity rises, mimicking the rain that triggers wild flushes. Growers manipulate these two factors to schedule harvests.",
    "Contamination is the beginner's main enemy. Sterile technique — clean hands, clean tools, and pasteurized substrates — prevents molds such as Trichoderma and Penicillium from outcompeting the mushroom mycelium. If a grow bag turns green or black, it is contaminated and should be disposed of away from the rest of the crop.",
    "Mushrooms are cultivated commercially on an industrial scale: the global mushroom industry produces tens of millions of tons a year, led by button mushrooms, shiitake and oyster mushrooms. Most of the production is concentrated in China, which grows more than half of the world's mushrooms.",
    "Cultivated mushrooms offer a zero-risk way to enjoy mushrooms compared with foraging, because the grower controls the species from spawn to harvest. The same cannot be said of wild mushrooms, which must be identified species by species.",
]

