COOKING = [
    "Mushrooms are about 90 percent water, so most cooking methods are about driving off moisture and concentrating flavor. High heat and a dry pan give mushrooms a golden-brown crust; simmering gives them a soft, slippery texture. The two styles suit different species and different dishes.",
    "Drying is the oldest way to preserve mushrooms. Porcini, morels and black trumpets dry superbly and develop an intense, concentrated flavor that returns when rehydrated in warm water. The soaking liquid is itself a prized stock, full of mushroom flavor.",
    "Never wash cultivated mushrooms if you can avoid it; a brush or a dry cloth removes most dirt without waterlogging the flesh. Wild mushrooms should be cleaned carefully, gill-side down, brushing away soil and checking for insects, before they are cooked the same day.",
    "Some edible wild mushrooms must be cooked before eating: morels, honey fungus and wood blewits contain heat-sensitive compounds that cause stomach upset when raw. Others, such as the cultivated enoki and button mushroom, are safe to eat raw in salads.",
    "Chanterelles are best cooked simply, in butter, with a little salt and pepper, so their apricot-like aroma survives. They release a lot of water at first; patience and a wide pan let the moisture evaporate and the flavor concentrate.",
    "The porcini is one of the most versatile edible mushrooms: it can be sliced and grilled, dried for risotto, pickled, or made into a sauce. The bitter bolete, its common lookalike, ruins any dish with intense bitterness, which is why the taste test is part of the identification routine.",
    "Mushrooms pair well with butter, cream, garlic, thyme, eggs, cheese and wine, and they are a staple of vegetarian cooking as a meat substitute because of their savory, umami-rich flavor. The umami comes from free amino acids such as glutamate, the same compound that gives soy sauce its savor.",
    "Morels should always be cooked thoroughly and in small batches; their hollow caps can hide soil and insects, so they need careful cleaning. Sautéed morels with cream are a classic spring dish in Europe and North America.",
    "Truffles are used raw or barely warmed, shaved thin over pasta, eggs or risotto, because cooking destroys their delicate aroma. A few grams of truffle can perfume an entire dish, which is why they are sold by weight at luxury prices.",
    "Mushrooms are nutritious: low in calories and fat, with useful amounts of protein, fiber, B vitamins, selenium, copper and potassium. Some species are rich in beta-glucans, a type of fiber studied for its effects on cholesterol and the immune system.",
    "Vitamin D is rare in food, but mushrooms are the exception: they produce vitamin D2 when exposed to ultraviolet light, and commercially grown mushrooms are sometimes UV-treated to boost their vitamin D content. Drying mushrooms in sunlight can increase it further.",
    "Storing fresh mushrooms: keep them in a paper bag in the refrigerator, unwashed, and use within a few days. Plastic bags trap moisture and speed up spoilage. Cooked mushrooms freeze well, and dried mushrooms keep for months in an airtight jar.",
]

