REGIONAL = [
    "In Europe, autumn is the great mushroom season: porcini, chanterelles, ceps and hedgehog mushrooms appear in beech and oak woods after the first rains. The same woods hold the death cap, so European foragers learn the Amanita family early.",
    "In North America, spring brings morels to burned forests, orchards and floodplains, while autumn brings chanterelles, porcini and the distinctive lobster mushroom. Lawns offer the green-spored parasol, the continent's most common poisoning source.",
    "In East Asia, the culture of mushrooms is ancient: shiitake, enoki, wood ear and snow fungus are everyday foods, matsutake is a luxury autumn gift, and reishi and chaga are traditional medicines. The poison fire coral is a local hazard unknown in the West.",
    "In the tropics, mushroom diversity is enormous but much less documented; edible and poisonous species overlap in markets, and identifications rely on local knowledge passed down through generations.",
    "In Australia and New Zealand, the death cap and fly agaric were introduced with European trees and now fruit under oaks and pines; local foragers must learn the introduced Amanitas alongside the native species.",
    "Mediterranean regions have their own mushroom culture: the Caesar's mushroom, the saffron milk cap and the prized black truffle, harvested with dogs in winter. The climate produces long autumn seasons and some of the most sought-after edible fungi in the world.",
    "Nordic forests offer chanterelles, ceps and the winter-fruiting velvet shank, and the tradition of public access (allemansrätten) makes foraging a national pastime. Identification standards are high, and field guides are a fixture of every home.",
    "High-altitude regions such as the Tibetan plateau are home to the caterpillar fungus, harvested each spring by entire communities. It is an extreme example of how fungi shape local economies and cultures.",
]

# --------------------------------------------------------------------- #
# mycology fundamentals (essays)
# --------------------------------------------------------------------- #
FUNDAMENTALS = [
    "A mushroom is the fruiting body of a fungus, the visible reproductive organ of an organism whose main body — the mycelium — lives hidden in soil, wood or other organic matter. The mycelium is a network of thread-like hyphae that can extend for enormous distances; the largest known organism on earth is a honey fungus mycelium covering several square kilometers of forest.",
    "Fungi are neither plants nor animals. They form their own kingdom, closer to animals than to plants in evolutionary terms. Unlike plants they cannot photosynthesize; they feed by secreting enzymes that break down organic matter and absorbing the released nutrients.",
    "Fungi play three ecological roles: saprotrophs decompose dead organic matter, parasites feed on living organisms, and mycorrhizal symbionts trade nutrients with plant roots. Mycorrhizal fungi supply plants with water and minerals in exchange for sugars, and roughly ninety percent of plant species depend on them.",
    "The mushroom itself exists to produce spores, the microscopic reproductive cells that disperse to new habitats. A single large puffball can release trillions of spores, and most species rely on wind, water or animals to carry them. Only a tiny fraction ever germinate.",
    "Mushroom reproduction has two stages. Spores germinate into a monokaryotic mycelium with one nucleus per cell; when two compatible monokaryons meet, they fuse into a dikaryotic mycelium with two nuclei per cell. Only the dikaryon is capable of fruiting, which is why single spores rarely produce mushrooms in cultivation.",
    "Taxonomy organizes fungi into ever finer groups: kingdom, phylum, class, order, family, genus, species. The two great phyla of mushrooms are the Basidiomycota, which includes most gilled mushrooms, boletes and polypores, and the Ascomycota, which includes morels, truffles and cup fungi. Modern classification increasingly relies on DNA sequences rather than appearance alone.",
]

